#
# D-142 — A RULE THAT CANNOT SAY WHAT IT SAW CANNOT BE ASKED WHETHER IT SAW.
#
# THE GAP: every structural rule here was an offender-only assertion over a
# derived membership — `assert not offenders`, or `== []` on a real module —
# with no anchor that the derivation had recognised any real member. Such a
# rule is green when the package is clean AND green when the taint tracker has
# silently stopped recognising the package's spelling, and those are opposite
# facts about the world.
#
# DRIVEN by TEST at d8215c5: with `_has_literal_taint` replaced by
# `lambda fn: False` — i.e. the scan recognises zero real manifest readers —
# `test_every_manifest_record_reader_in_the_package_establishes_the_shape`
# PASSED, as did `test_the_provider_module_passes_its_own_package_wide_rule`,
# `test_the_isinstance_route_clears_the_real_intent_coverage_reader`, and
# `test_the_manifest_scan_looks_in_both_shipped_source_trees` (which counted
# MODULES, >20, not readers — a count a blind tracker does not change).
#
# THIS IS NOT HYPOTHETICAL. The suite's own comment records that exactly this
# failure mode fired during GRIND-20, when the manifest reads moved behind a
# `(value, problem)` primitive: every taint chain in the module started at a
# tuple target, the scan found NOTHING, and only a vacuity assertion stood
# between that and a green test asserting nothing at all.
#
# THE FIX HAS TWO HALVES, and the second is what makes the probe possible.
#
#   (a) every scan returns (seen, offenders), and each rule asserts `seen`
#       names the sites the defect was filed on — the seven readers of
#       _D134_FILED_READERS for the package rule, the three foundry_spawn.py
#       readers for the provider rule, load_manifest_casting_ids for the
#       isinstance rule, and a PLANTED member of both rules for the roots rule.
#
#   (b) literal taint now enters through ONE primitive. It used to enter in
#       three places — `_has_literal_taint`, `_taint_map`'s assignment branch
#       and `_returning_taint`'s return branch — each with its own inline copy
#       of the same `n.value == "manifest.json"` test. Three copies is three
#       chances for the derivation to go blind in one place and stay green
#       everywhere else, and it is why the probe could not be aimed at a single
#       seam. Now there is one seam, and blinding it blinds everything.
#
# THE ROOTS RULE COUNTS MEMBERS, NOT MODULES — by PLANTING one rather than
# censusing what the trees hold. A census cannot be written honestly here: the
# hyphenated scripts tree held four decode sites this morning and holds none
# now (D-141 routed them through the primitive), and it has never held a
# manifest reader, so a census over it would have to assert zero — the vacuity
# the rule is trying to escape. A planted member is found only if the root is
# really walked and the predicates really recognise it. The plant goes into a
# TEMPORARY root driven through the shipped `_scanned_modules` rglob, because
# two other castings run this suite against the same working tree and a module
# that exists for four seconds inside src/foundry_mcp is a spurious offender in
# somebody else's run. What the real roots owe — that they exist and are walked
# EXHAUSTIVELY — is asserted against them directly, by an independent os.walk.
#
# THE FOUR PARAMETRISED ROWS BELOW ARE THE REGRESSION: each calls the SHIPPED
# rule (not a copy of it) under the blind probe and requires it to raise, with
# a message that says the derivation went blind rather than that the package
# has a finding. All four passed under this probe before; none does now.

